ARTICLE I — THE FIRST AXIOM

Axiom I.1 — Human Health Is an Optimization Problem

Let:

[
H_i(t)
]

represent the health state of individual (i) at time (t).

Then:

[
H_i(t+1)=F(H_i(t),G_i,E_i,B_i,L_i,N_i,T_i,\epsilon_i)
]

where:

* (G) = genetics
* (E) = environment
* (B) = biomarkers
* (L) = lifestyle
* (N) = nutrition
* (T) = treatment
* (\epsilon) = biological randomness and unobserved variables.

Therefore no universal treatment function can perfectly describe every human.

[
\boxed{H_i \neq H_j}
]

in general.

⸻

ARTICLE II — THE PERSONALIZATION AXIOM

Axiom II.1

For every individual (i), treatment should be evaluated conditional upon the individual’s measurable characteristics.

[
P(O|D,i,C)
]

is the probability of outcome (O) given:

* drug/intervention (D),
* individual (i),
* condition (C).

The preferred intervention is:

[
D^*

\arg\max_D
\left[
P(Benefit|D,i,C)

\lambda P(Harm|D,i,C)
\right].
]

Thus:

[
\boxed{
\text{Personalized medicine}>\text{undifferentiated medicine}
}
]

whenever sufficient evidence exists to justify personalization.

⸻

ARTICLE III — THE 100% PRINCIPLE

Mathematical Futurological Medicine does not assert that every disease can presently be cured with probability 1.

Instead it establishes a stronger methodological objective:

[
\boxed{
P(\text{correct medical decision})\rightarrow1
}
]

through continuous measurement, prediction, experimentation, and model updating.

The distinction is fundamental.

Proposition

Biological outcomes cannot generally be guaranteed.

Therefore

[
P(\text{cure})<1
]

may remain unavoidable.

But

[
P(\text{appropriate decision})
]

can theoretically approach 1 as information, diagnostics, prediction, and treatment-selection algorithms improve.

