ARTICLE IX — CHEMICAL LOGIC

MFM defines a chemical reaction abstractly as:

[
R:
{S_1,S_2,\ldots,S_n}
\rightarrow
{P_1,P_2,\ldots,P_m}
]

where (S) represents reactants and (P) represents products.

A reaction is accepted as chemically valid only if relevant conservation constraints hold.

Mass conservation

[
\sum_j m(S_j)

\sum_k m(P_k)
]

Atomic conservation

For every element (e):

[
\sum_j n_e(S_j)

\sum_k n_e(P_k).
]

Charge conservation

[
\sum_j q(S_j)

\sum_k q(P_k).
]

A generic reaction therefore becomes:

[
\boxed{
Reactants
\land
Conditions
\land
Conservation
\Rightarrow
Products
}
]

The arrow (\rightarrow) represents a transformation, not an assertion that the transformation will occur under arbitrary conditions.

