ARTICLE XII — THE MANUFACTURING ASSUMPTION REGISTRY

A complete pharmaceutical manufacturing model shall explicitly identify, at minimum, the following assumptions.

ASSUMPTION A1 — Identity

Major Premise:

Every manufacturing process requires correctly identified starting materials.

Minor Premise:

The selected starting material has been independently identified.

Conclusion:

Therefore the starting material may be admitted into the modeled process.

[
A1:
Identity(S)=Verified
]

⸻

ASSUMPTION A2 — Purity

Major Premise:

Material identity alone does not establish chemical purity.

Minor Premise:

The starting material possesses measured purity within the required specification.

Conclusion:

Therefore purity is acceptable for the modeled process.

[
A2:
Purity(S)\geq P_{min}
]

⸻

ASSUMPTION A3 — Stoichiometry

Major Premise:

A chemically balanced transformation requires conservation of atoms.

Minor Premise:

The proposed reaction satisfies elemental balance.

Conclusion:

Therefore the proposed reaction is stoichiometrically admissible.

[
A3:
Balance(R)=True
]

⸻

ASSUMPTION A4 — Reaction Validity

Major Premise:

A theoretically balanced equation does not prove that a reaction actually occurs.

Minor Premise:

Independent experimental evidence establishes the transformation.

Conclusion:

Therefore the reaction may be treated as experimentally supported.

[
A4:
Evidence(R)>Threshold
]

⸻

ASSUMPTION A5 — Process Control

Major Premise:

Manufacturing outcomes depend upon controlled process variables.

Minor Premise:

The relevant variables are monitored within validated ranges.

Conclusion:

Therefore process variability is constrained.

[
A5:
Var(Process)\leq V_{max}
]

⸻

ASSUMPTION A6 — Product Identity

Major Premise:

A product cannot be assumed to be the intended compound merely because a reaction was performed.

Minor Premise:

Analytical evidence confirms product identity.

Conclusion:

Therefore the product identity is established.

[
A6:
Identity(P)=Verified
]

⸻

ASSUMPTION A7 — Purity of Final Product

Major Premise:

A biologically active compound may contain contaminants or degradation products.

Minor Premise:

Analytical testing establishes conformity with predefined specifications.

Conclusion:

Therefore the product satisfies the modeled purity requirement.

[
A7:
Purity(P)\geq P_{spec}
]

⸻

ASSUMPTION A8 — Stability

Major Premise:

A product that degrades unpredictably cannot be assumed to deliver a stable dose.

Minor Premise:

Stability evidence establishes acceptable behavior under specified storage conditions.

Conclusion:

Therefore the modeled product has demonstrated stability under those conditions.

[
A8:
Stability(P,t)\geq S_{min}
]

⸻

ASSUMPTION A9 — Dose

Major Premise:

Pharmacological effects depend upon exposure.

Minor Premise:

The administered dose produces a characterized exposure.

Conclusion:

Therefore dose-response analysis is possible.

[
A9:
Exposure=f(Dose,Absorption,Distribution,Metabolism,Excretion)
]

⸻

ASSUMPTION A10 — Biological Response

Major Premise:

Pharmacological exposure does not guarantee a desired clinical response.

Minor Premise:

Clinical evidence establishes a probability of response.

Conclusion:

Therefore treatment efficacy is represented probabilistically.

[
A10:
0\leq P(Response|D,i,C)\leq1.
]

